<h1>Mechanics II: symmetry is conservation</h1>
<p class="meta">August 22, 2026</p>

<p>
Physical systems tend to obey <em>conservation laws</em>. A conservation law is just an assertion that, in some physical system, some quantity never changes, no matter how the system evolves. Let's see some examples.
</p>

<h3>Conservation of energy</h3>

<p>
Imagine you have a ball of mass \(m.\) You are standing on top of a very tall building, and drop your ball from a very large height \(H\) above the ground. Let's ignore wind resistance, so that the only force acting on this ball is gravity, and try to see how your ball falls to the ground over time.
</p>

<p>
Say at time \(t\), your ball has height \(h(t).\) So at time \(t=0\) (the moment of the drop), we have \(h(0) = H.\) 
</p>

<p>
How much energy does the ball have at time \(t\)? Recall that the total energy is the sum of potential energy and kinetic energy. As we discussed last time, the potential energy coming from gravity is
\[P(t) = mgh(t),\]
where \(g \approx 10 \frac{\text{m}}{\text{s}^2}\) is the acceleration due to gravity on Earth (we hope you're on Earth -- if not, use your planet's gravitational constant). 
</p>

<p>
The kinetic energy depends on the velocity; specifically, 
\[K(t) = \frac{1}{2}mv(t)^2.\]
Luckily, gravity acts with constant acceleration \(g,\) so your ball's velocity at time \(t\) will be \(-gt\) (the negative sign is because the ball is falling down). Thus it has kinetic energy
\[K(t) = \frac{1}{2} m(gt)^2.\] 
</p>

<p>
Thus the total energy at time \(t\) is 
\[E(t) = P(t) + K(t) = mgh(t) + \frac{1}{2}mg^2t^2.\] 
</p>

<p>
However, we can compute \(h(t)\) exactly: because \(h'(t) = v(t),\) and we know \(v(t) = -gt,\) we have
\[h(t) = H - \frac{1}{2}gt^2\]
by some basic calculus, recalling that \(h(0) = H\) was the height of the building we dropped the ball from.
</p>

<p>
Thus
\[E(t) = mgH - \frac{1}{2}mg^2t^2 + \frac{1}{2}mg^2t^2 = mgH.\] 
In particular, the energy is <em>independent of time!</em>
</p>

<p>
This is a standard example of a conservation law: the energy does not change no matter how much time passes, so we say that the energy \(E(t)\) is a conserved quantity. 
</p>

<p>
We'll remark that this toy physical model does break down: after a long enough time, your ball will hit the Earth, and lose some energy in the collision; so, this conservation law only applies if we stop time <em>before</em> our ball hits the Earth. To get a more permanent conservation law, you'd need to account for the kinetic energy of the Earth. 
</p>

<h3>Conservation of momentum</h3>

<p>
In the last example, we cut off time before your ball hit the Earth; to give you a hint of how you'd account for that, let's give an example of a physical system involving collisions. 
</p>

<p>
Imagine you are playing billiards. There is a billiard ball of mass \(m_1\) resting on the table. You hit the cue ball (say of mass \(m_2\)) in the direction of that billiard ball, and they collide; let's say that, just before the collision, the cue ball has velocity \(v.\) What happens to the balls? 
</p>

<p>
In the previous example, we used Newton's laws to derive a formula for the total energy, and then observed that energy is conserved. However, the true power of conservation laws is that you can use them as a <em>shortcut</em> -- if you know some quantity is conserved, then you don't need to fully study your physical system.
</p>

<p>
Let's say our table is frictionless. Then all of the energy in our balls is kinetic, and so a moment before collision, the 2-ball system has total energy
\[E = \frac{1}{2}m_2v^2 + \frac{1}{2}m_1 \cdot 0 = \frac{1}{2}m_2v^2.\]
</p>

<p>
Let's assume that, like in the previous system, <em>energy is conserved</em>. Later, we will derive 
</p>

<p>After the collision, the billiard ball I hit is moving at another velocity \(v_2\), and the billiard ball which was sitting on the table is now moving at some velocity \(v_1\). 
  The total momentum here is \(m_1v_1+m_2v_2\).</p>

  <p>
    By conservation of momentum, we know the total momentum is conserved, so
    \[
    m_2v=m_1v_1+m_2v_2.
    \]
    If you combine this with the conservation of energy,
    \[
    \frac12m_2v^2=\frac12m_1v_1^2+\frac12m_2v_2^2,
    \]
    you can solve for \(v_1\) and \(v_2\)!
  </p>


<h3>Conservation of angular momentum</h3>

  Angular momentum is defined with respect to some origin--the "center of rotation".
For a particle with momentum \(\vec p\) and position vector \(\vec r\), the angular momentum is the cross product \(\vec r\times\vec p\).

  <p>
    For example, say there is a particle of mass \(m\) rotating in a circle of radius \(r\) around a rod with frequency \(f\).
    Then the particle is moving with velocity \(2\pi fr\), so the momentum is \(2\pi mfr\). Therefore, the angular momentum has size
    \[2\pi mfr^2.\]
    Let's say the particle moves into a smaller orbit, of radius \(r'<{r}\). That particle will have a new frequency \(f'\).                                                              Then conservation of angular momentum tells us
    \[2\pi mfr^2=2\pi mf'(r')^2.\]
    We can solve for \(f'\) as
    \[f'=\frac{r^2}{(r')^2}f>f.\]
    In other words, as the radius decreases, the frequency increases!
      This is the reason why when figure skaters pull in their arms, <a href="https://www.youtube.com/shorts/4b7v3Yki4os">they rotate more frequently</a>.
  </p>
  
  <p>Each of our conserved quantities (energy, momentum, and angular momentum) tells us something useful about our physical system.
    However, finding these quantities requires some cleverness on our part. 
    Wouldn't it be wonderful if there is a more systematic method for finding conserved quantities?
    Emmy Noether discovered that conserved quantities exactly correspond to continuous symmetries of the physical system, thereby answering this question!
  </p>

<h2>What is continuous symmetry?</h2>
A windmill as below has symmetries.

<img src="windmill.svg" alt="TikZ graph" style="display:block;
            margin:2rem auto;
            max-width:90%;
            height:auto;">

For example, rotating the picture by \(90^\circ\) doesn't change the picture. Neither does rotation by \(180^\circ\) or \(270^\circ\). Of course rotating by \(0^\circ\), which does nothing, also doesn't change the shape! 
However, if you rotate by, say, \(30^\circ\), the blades will be at a different position. So we would say the windmill has 4 symmetries: rotation by \(0^\circ\), \(90^\circ\), \(180^\circ\), and \(270^\circ\).

<p>On the other hand, consider a circle. </p>

<img src="circle.svg" alt="TikZ graph" style="display:block;
            margin:2rem auto;
            max-width:90%;
            height:auto;">
Then, rotating by any angle \(\theta\) doesn't change the shape. That means the circle has infinitely many symmetries! That's why we would say a circle has a <em>continuous</em> set of symmetries, while the windmill only has a <em>discrete</em> set of symmetries.


<p>
  Our world has three types of continuous symmertries:
  <ul>
  <li>Time symmetry: our universe will have the same physical laws 10 seconds from now.</li>
  <li>Translation symmetry: the laws of physics on my block is the same as the laws of physics 3 blocks down.</li>
  <li>Rotational symmetry: if I turn left, the laws of physics don't change.</li>
</ul>
Noether tells us that each of these symmetries corresponds to a conserved quantity.</p>
